import { EffectSequencingCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> EffectSequencingCopy = EffectSequencingCopy {
  summary: "Construct the operation afresh each time it is executed.",
  taskTitle: "Wait until execution to call the preparation function",
  purpose: "Delay the call that prepares a preview string until work is executed, rather than calling it when work is created. Executing the same work twice performs the preparation twice.",
  typescriptBefore: "In TypeScript, store a function that calls prepare. Creating that function does not call prepare; each work() call does. This example needs no Promise.",
  benefit: "defer expresses this construction-at-execution boundary as an operation that can be composed with others. Keeping the operation and calling its preparation function become separate steps.",
  setup: "The std/effect import gives the helpers the effects prefix. prepare is an ordinary function that takes a String and joins text with +. Its call is the work we want to delay.",
  after: [
    "The \\_ -> ... passed to effects.defer is a function to call later, or a callback. The _ marks an unused argument. defer does not call it immediately: each execution of work calls it to obtain the operation for that execution.",
    "Inside the callback, prepare runs and its result becomes the argument to effects.succeed. let work stores an operation with these instructions, not an already prepared string. defer does not save the result for reuse.",
    "The do block in effect fn main executes from top to bottom. _ <- println waits for output and discards its result. first <- work and second <- work each execute work and receive that execution's successful value.",
    "Both preparations use the same input, so the two preview lines are identical. Identical output alone does not establish when a function was called or whether a result was reused. Calling the callback on every execution is defer's behavior.",
    "In the following unexecuted example, divide takes an Int integer and divides 7 by it. _work names an intentionally unused operation. The call that divides by zero is inside the callback, so constructing _work does not evaluate it.",
    "That example prints Constructed without executing without running _work. Executing _work would then cause division by zero. Delaying execution does not make an unsafe calculation safe."
  ],
  details: [
    "The callback type shown below is Unit -> Effect<R, E, A>. Unit has the value (), which supplies no meaningful input. defer passes that value to the callback; the _ parameter ignores it.",
    "A common mistake is expecting effects.succeed (prepare title) to delay the prepare call by itself. Its argument is evaluated first when that succeed expression is evaluated. To delay the call too, put it inside defer's callback as shown.",
    "defer is not a cache. Executing the same work twice calls its callback twice. Reusing a value computed only once requires a separate decision about where to keep that value.",
    "Deferring a runtime defect such as division by zero does not turn it into an expected, typed failure that attempt, recover, or mapError can handle. For a recoverable input error, check the input before calculating and return an expected failure explicitly."
  ]
}
